# device_workflow.options — nanopb caps for the DEVICE workflow-task subset
# (proto/mwf/device_workflow.proto). Precedence: LAST matching line wins, so the
# wildcard defaults go first and specifics below.
#
# NOTE ON SHARED TYPES: device_workflow.proto IMPORTS device_activity.proto for
# Payload/Payloads/WorkflowExecution/Workflow-/ActivityType/TaskQueue/Failure.
# Those structs are generated (and CAPPED) by device_activity.options during the
# device_activity generation pass — nanopb only references them here, it does not
# regenerate them, so their caps live in device_activity.options, NOT this file.
# This file caps ONLY device_workflow.proto's OWN string/bytes/repeated fields.
#
# ── Static-footprint accounting (why the caps are what they are) ──────────────
# A HistoryEvent's oneof is a C union sized to its largest arm. The Payloads-
# carrying arms dominate: Payloads = 4 × Payload, each Payload = metadata[8]
# (~1.6 KB) + data[8192] (device_activity caps) ≈ 9.8 KB, so Payloads ≈ 39 KB and
# a HistoryEvent ≈ 39 KB. History.events[64] ⇒ History ≈ 2.5 MB, and
# PollWorkflowTaskQueueResponse ≈ 2.5 MB. That is HEAP-allocated per poll — on
# the device that heap is PSRAM (8 MB, heap_caps_malloc_extmem_enable), NEVER the
# stack/statics (same rule as the activity subset). To shrink for tighter RAM,
# lower History.events max_count or device_activity's Payload.data cap.

# ── Global defaults (matched first, overridden below) ─────────────────────────
# ⚠ 256 B (was 64) — HARDWARE-CAUGHT (ESP32-S3): a REAL Mistral history carries a
# 64-hex-char EXECUTION id as workflow_id (measured 64 chars in
# probe/workflow_task_1.json), and nanopb reserves 1 B for the
# NUL, so max_size:64 held only 63 chars → PollWorkflowTaskQueueResponse aborted
# the whole poll with "string overflow". The golden histories use SHORT synthetic
# ids ("linear-goldencapture-0001") so the golden-only conformance test never hit
# it. This default now covers device_workflow's OWN string fields — most notably
# WorkflowExecutionStartedEventAttributes.workflow_id (field 28) — plus run/request
# ids (UUID 36 B) and server identity strings ("1@api-workflows-…", 32 B): real max
# 64 → 256 = 4× headroom. These structs are PSRAM-heap-allocated per poll (see the
# footnote), so the extra bytes are cheap; correctness wins.
* max_size:256
* max_count:4

# ── ⚠ SHARED-TYPE caps MUST MIRROR device_activity.options ────────────────────
# device_workflow imports Payload/Payloads/Failure from device_activity. nanopb
# computes each static-submessage field's WIDTH (PB_BIND width, data_size bits)
# at GENERATION time from the caps IT sees in THIS pass — but the field's REAL
# struct is the one compiled from device_activity.pb.h. If the two disagree,
# nanopb hardcodes too-narrow a width (e.g. width-2 data_size=12bit=4 KB) and the
# generated device_workflow.pb.c fails to compile against the real 8 KB Payload
# (FIELDINFO_DOES_NOT_FIT). So these lines are copied verbatim from
# device_activity.options and MUST stay in sync with it.
mwf.device.v1.Payload.data                     max_size:8192
mwf.device.v1.Payload.metadata                 max_count:8
mwf.device.v1.Payload.MetadataEntry.key        max_size:64
mwf.device.v1.Payload.MetadataEntry.value      max_size:128
mwf.device.v1.Payloads.payloads                max_count:4
mwf.device.v1.Failure.message                  max_size:512
mwf.device.v1.Failure.stack_trace              max_size:256
mwf.device.v1.Failure.source                   max_size:64
# Shared IDENTITY types embedded in device_workflow messages (WorkflowExecution
# in PollWFTQResponse.field2; WorkflowType/ActivityType/TaskQueue in event arms).
# nanopb sizes each embedded submessage field from the caps IT sees in THIS pass,
# so these MUST match what device_activity.options compiled the real struct to, or
# the widths disagree (FIELDINFO_DOES_NOT_FIT). WorkflowExecution.workflow_id is the
# 64-hex Mistral execution id (measured 64 B) → 256; the rest are UUIDs/short names.
mwf.device.v1.WorkflowExecution.workflow_id    max_size:256
mwf.device.v1.WorkflowExecution.run_id         max_size:128
mwf.device.v1.WorkflowType.name                max_size:128
mwf.device.v1.ActivityType.name                max_size:128
mwf.device.v1.TaskQueue.name                   max_size:128

# ── History: a v1 workflow history is small; 64 events covers the goldens with
#    headroom (linear = 17 events). This is the multiplicative knob — see above. ─
mwf.device.v1.History.events                                          max_count:64

# ── Task tokens / page tokens are opaque server bytes; give them room. ─────────
# next_page_token stays capped despite the single-page v1 assumption: a non-empty
# value must be DECODABLE so the worker can detect+refuse a paged task, not
# silently truncate the history.
mwf.device.v1.PollWorkflowTaskQueueResponse.task_token               max_size:256
mwf.device.v1.PollWorkflowTaskQueueResponse.next_page_token          max_size:256
mwf.device.v1.RespondWorkflowTaskCompletedRequest.task_token         max_size:256

# ── namespace is "<uuid>:<uuid>" = 73 chars on the Mistral frontend → 128. ─────
mwf.device.v1.PollWorkflowTaskQueueRequest.namespace_                max_size:128
mwf.device.v1.PollWorkflowTaskQueueRequest.identity                  max_size:128
mwf.device.v1.RespondWorkflowTaskCompletedRequest.namespace_         max_size:128
mwf.device.v1.RespondWorkflowTaskCompletedRequest.identity           max_size:128

# ── Commands a single workflow task emits: a handful (schedule N activities,
#    start timers, then complete/fail). 16 covers fan-out with headroom. ────────
mwf.device.v1.RespondWorkflowTaskCompletedRequest.commands           max_count:16

# ── SignalWorkflowExecutionRequest (ClientOps::signalWorkflow). Its OWN strings;
# workflow_execution.workflow_id (the 64-hex Mistral execution id) + input Payloads
# are shared types capped above. namespace "<uuid>:<uuid>"=73 → 128; signal_name is
# author-defined (short) but 128 gives ample headroom; identity like the poll req. ─
mwf.device.v1.SignalWorkflowExecutionRequest.namespace_              max_size:128
mwf.device.v1.SignalWorkflowExecutionRequest.signal_name             max_size:128
mwf.device.v1.SignalWorkflowExecutionRequest.identity                max_size:128
